Description: hw/input: Add the TI TSC2100 touchscreen controller and audio codec
 The TSC2100's SPI interface: register pages for conversion results,
 touch control and audio control, conversions that finish at once, and
 the PINTDAV output. The pen is QEMU's absolute pointer.
 .
 The TSC2100 is the Chumby Classic's audio codec as well as its
 touchscreen controller: the stock kernel's chumby-tsc2100 ALSA driver
 (also /dev/dsp through the OSS emulation) programs its volume and mutes
 in the DAC volume register and its power in Power Control, its clocks
 in the PLL and Audio Control 1 and 3, and runs it as the master of the
 I2S link to SSI1. The control panel keeps a 44.1 kHz stream open from
 boot.
 .
 The model is the master of a serial audio link that the board
 provides: while powered up it clocks frames at the DAC's rate through
 the link, hands those it plays to the audio backend as 16-bit stereo at
 that rate, with the DAC's volume and mutes, and feeds the link the
 backend's recording at the same rate (silence when the backend has
 none). The backend voices are only active while something drives
 (takes) the link.
 .
 The rate is Fsref divided by Audio Control 1's DACFS field, and Fsref
 comes from MCLK: through the PLL as MCLK * J.D / (2048 * P) when
 PLL1.PLLSEL is set, or as MCLK / (128 * Q) without it. The codec drives
 the link only as master (CONTROL3.SLVMS). The stock driver programs P,
 J and D for the 16 MHz oscillator of hardware 1.6 and later, and sets
 SLVMS; the master clock is an "mclk" property defaulting to 16 MHz, so
 a driver that misprograms the PLL or leaves the codec a slave hears it.
 The stock firmware's playback runs at 44.1 kHz (hw_ptr advancing 176000
 frames in 4 s).
 .
 Frames are clocked at most 4 ms at a time when the audio timer runs
 late, the rest slipping, as an overloaded host loses sound rather than
 bursting it. Catching up 100 ms at once ran the DMA past two of the
 stock driver's 8 KiB periods before the period interrupt was handled,
 and its handler, which busy-waits for the next period to have just
 started, timed out ("timed-out wating for DMA to start").
 .
 The recording voice is opened the first time the guest records at a
 rate, not with the playback voice, and a backend that can't record
 (CoreAudio, the default on macOS) is asked only once per rate:
 recording then reads silence.
 .
 The register address loaded from a migration stream must be below 64:
 it picks one of the 64 registers in a page and is used before it is
 masked.
 .
 The frame-rate model is derived from one written alongside the Linux
 5.9 TSC2100 driver.
